    "evidence_text": "Genetics of obesity in adult adoptees and their biological siblings.\n\nGenetic analysis of human obesity in an Italian sample.\n\nOur conclusions are that genetic factors are certainly present.\n\nSeveral analyses suggest the presence of a dominant major gene with weak effect.\n\nObesity as a medical problem.\n\nCongenital leptin deficiency is associated with severe early-onset obesity in humans.\n\nThe links between obesity in parents and their offspring and the role of genes and a shared environment are not completely understood.\n\nMaternal obesity epigenetically alters visceral fat progenitor cell properties in male offspring mice.\n\nKEY POINTS Maternal obesity reduces adipogenic progenitor density in offspring adipose tissue.\n\nProgenitor cells are the source of new adipocytes.\n\nFamilial obesity and leanness.\n\nObesity is a multifactorial disease with a marked genetic component.\n\nFurthermore, the distribution of fat shows a stronger heritable tendency compared with total body fat.\n\nNo such associations were found with the other candidate genes studied.\n\nAn adoption study of human obesity.\n\nWe conclude that genetic influences have an important role in determining human fatness in adults, whereas the family environment alone has no apparent effect.\n\nDietary fat and obesity: evidence from epidemiology.\n\nProspective studies of diet in relation to subsequent weight change give inconsistent results.",
